A||||7||Rodrv6||Filling the gas tank||||5||
Z||000000||Rodrv6||05-24-2000||04:11 PM||||Ever since my 84 was brand new I've noticed that when I am pumping gas into the tank and the nozzle shuts off, the tank is not full!  If I go really slowly I can get 3 more gallons in there before it finally fills completely.  Once I even emptied the tank competely to see what the capacity really was.  After 7.6 gallons went in the nozzle shut off automatically and I had to drizzle in the last 3 gallons to get the tank to hold the 10.6 gallons that the specs say it should.  I've noticed the same thing on a couple of other 84's.  I've checked all my fill and vent lines for blockage but have found no problems.  I've even had the tank out a couple of times trying to find something wrong with no luck.  Does anyone else have this problem?  Is it only on the 84's or is it just me?  With such a small tank to begin with I really like to use all the space I can!  TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-24-2000||04:23 PM||||It's not just you - when I ran out of gas I could only get something like 8.6 gallons in there before the pump shut off.  And I have an 88 which is supposed to hold more than 11 gallons!<P>I've never tried just continuing to dribble gas in there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||olympic||05-24-2000||04:47 PM||||   Many cars have this problem.  I had an old Ford Tempo and I could squeeze in about 4-5 gallons after the pump clicked off!!  It's just a lesson in poor design from the factory.  Either the inlet in the tank is not the highest point or the vent lines are not in the right spot.<BR>  Some of the newer cars are better.  I've noticed that some of them(Taurus, Villager, Suzuki Esteem for example) will only take about a litre(quart) more fuel after the pump clicks off and they will be full right up to the top of  the filler neck.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-24-2000||05:33 PM||||this can be a simple as the angle the nozzle rests at or not pumping at full speed. instead of leaving it hang pull up on the handle while it fills. On my Pinto I had to rotate most nozzles 45 degrees or the thing would not fill at all. I hardly have to play with the caravan at all. Some pumps give the fiero a pain if you run them full speed other are fine just hanging there.<P>it all depends on the specific nozzle, how fast the fuel flows, and just how the car's fill neck is bent. Some necks backup with foam really easy. The foam gets picked up by the shutoff tube and the nozzle thinks the car is full. The nozzle is doing exactly what it should, it's just that it's being lied to by the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||05-24-2000||05:46 PM||||I have an 85 GT, specs say 10.2 gallon tank. I have also experienced the "Dribble" theory and decided to find out how far it would go. I was on a Fiero run and need to top off for the long trip anyway. I put ALMOST 11 GALLONS IN IT! I was blown away! I must have filled the tank and the filler tube on top of that! <P>Drawback: I can't help but wonder how much of it sloshed out past my gas cap (I had the gas level at the cap!) on to the street on my first right turn....||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-25-2000||11:18 AM||||unless your cap is bad nothing should come out. Don't do that again tho. You can flood raw fuel into/thu the vapor canister. At the least you make the car run bad and ruin the canister but you can start a fire if it gets really flooded.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||05-25-2000||11:40 PM||||Just sitting here thinking about how many fuel stops I could have eliminated.<BR>About 20% it sounds like,but oh well,pretty soon the 3.4 will be in there and it'll be the same even with topping off.||||reg||1
Z||000007||wxxo||05-26-2000||11:09 AM||||ok heres one. i ran out of gas and could only get 8 gal in even though i know about the whole dribble thing. fact is that i can never get more than 8 in. even when i know that its empty. im beginning to wonder if there is a dead animal in the gas tank taking up all the extra room. haha. anyhow my thought on this is that there is such a thing as unusable fuel on airplanes. a cessna 152 will hold something like 26 gallons but can only use 24.5 of it. does anyone think that there could be a relation between the two. (i know its not the cessna forum...it seems relavant though.) i cant figure this one out. guys at the garage say that the fuel gauge is aok?? i dono. getting tired of filling up every 150miles. tia||||reg||1
